    "['ip [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set forwarding-options enhanced-hash-key family inet', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set forwarding-options enhanced-hash-key family inet', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset forwarding-options enhanced-hash-key family inet \\nset forwarding-options hash-key family mpls-label-1 \\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Cisco \"ip cef\" command enables Cisco Express Forwarding, which is Juniper\\'s default forwarding mechanism. However, to fully match CEF\\'s behavior in Juniper, we typically configure enhanced forwarding options including hash keys for inet and MPLS families, as Juniper uses different hashing mechanisms by default.\\n\\nNote: In most modern Juniper MX and PTX platforms, similar forwarding performance is achieved by default without explicit configuration, but these commands provide the closest functional equivalent to Cisco\\'s CEF.', 'trans_template': 'set forwarding-options enhanced-hash-key family inet'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set forwarding-options hash-key family mpls-label-1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set forwarding-options hash-key family mpls-label-1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset forwarding-options enhanced-hash-key family inet \\nset forwarding-options hash-key family mpls-label-1 \\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Cisco \"ip cef\" command enables Cisco Express Forwarding, which is Juniper\\'s default forwarding mechanism. However, to fully match CEF\\'s behavior in Juniper, we typically configure enhanced forwarding options including hash keys for inet and MPLS families, as Juniper uses different hashing mechanisms by default.\\n\\nNote: In most modern Juniper MX and PTX platforms, similar forwarding performance is achieved by default without explicit configuration, but these commands provide the closest functional equivalent to Cisco\\'s CEF.', 'trans_template': 'set forwarding-options hash-key family mpls-label-1'}]]": 1,
